title ALES116 — Lotus's stereo_replace, the general span-edit carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep
prompt recur the itinerary Lindy-first crux-first; after ALES115 sent, take the next agent-doable Lotus lap, self-approve, prove GREEN, send as its own signed round; STOP at custody gates
think ALES115 named the general span-edit replace for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) — two mono Clips heard together, one per speaker — whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the SAME length so the channels stay aligned in time. A keeper who pastes a corrected take over a selection pastes it into BOTH channels at once. Chosen crux-first because lifting the family's general case (replace) into stereo makes the whole mono edit family ALES111–115 usable on real stereo masters — the twin lifts them all. Carries replace into stereo the way ALES29 carried the insert-join: run the proven mono replace per channel with the same at and count.
think Shape: stereo_replace(sc, at, count, left_src, right_src) reuses ALES115's replace per channel over its public API, no new primitive. The stereo discipline is validate-BOTH-before-either: a stereo payload names one span across both channels, so left_src.len must equal right_src.len (else BadRange, inconsistent spans); then the mono preconditions (BadRange on a bad span, ClipFull against the headroom max_clip - len + count) are checked ONCE against the shared channel length up front. Because both channels enter equal-length and the payloads are equal-length, the two channels' checks are identical, so once every check passes each mono replace is pre-validated to succeed and no refusal can land between the two channel edits — the channels can never desynchronise.
think Four laws: the STEREO REPLACE law (left equals mono replace(left, ...) and right equals mono replace(right, ...), each byte for byte, proven side by side with differing per-channel payloads); the BALANCE / INVARIANT law (left.len == right.len after, the stereo image aligned, proven shorter/longer/equal); the ATOMICITY law — the crux of a two-channel edit: any refusal leaves BOTH channels untouched, proven for mismatched payloads, a bad span, and a ClipFull overflow (both channels filled to max_clip, buf[0] unchanged on each); the DEGENERATE / GENERALIZATION law (empty payloads count 0 the identity on both channels, and per channel the paste_over face payload-len == count holds).
obs GREEN on the first pass — no build red this time (the max_clip-not-buf.len lesson from ALES115 carried forward; headroom read timeline.max_clip directly). All four laws pass on metal, the atomicity law proven three ways including the pre-mutation ClipFull. The teaching crux is atomicity: a two-channel edit that mutates left then discovers right refuses would desync the master, so every check runs before either channel moves — equal lengths and equal-length payloads make the single shared check sound for both. Reuses ALES115's replace and ALES2's EditError whole, invents no primitive and no error (mismatched payloads fold into BadRange as inconsistent spans). >=2 positive-invariant asserts per fn; width-check clean; TAME style bans clean, ratchet zero-assert count unchanged. No custody gate touched — two pure-Rye local i16 Clips, siloed to lotus/.
